## STATEFUL vs STATELESS ARCHITECTURE ?
===========================================

Pichle chapter (Scaling) me humne dekha ki Horizontal Scaling ke baad
application ke multiple instances run karte hain.

Agar user ki session information kisi ek server ki local memory (RAM)
me store ho, to Load Balancer user ki next request kisi doosre server
par bhej sakta hai.

Us server ke paas previous session information available nahi hogi,
jisse session-related problems create ho sakti hain.

Is problem ko properly samajhne ke liye pehle hume
Stateful aur Stateless Architecture samajhna hoga.

-------------------
## WHAT IS STATE ?
-------------------
State simply means information that a system remembers and uses while processing future requests.


-----------------------
## WHAT IS STATELESS ?
-----------------------

Stateless application previous requests ki information remember nahi karti.

Har request independently process hoti hai, aur us request ko process karne ke liye required saari information usi request ke saath aati hai.

HTTP / HTTPS protocol bhi by default Stateless hota hai.

Matlab server ko by default previous requests yaad nahi rehti.

Phir question aata hai:

Agar HTTP / HTTPS Stateless hai, to user ko har request par dobara login kyun nahi karna padta?

Agar server har request ko completely independent process kare,

to user ko har request ke saath apne credentials dobara bhejne padenge.

Real-world applications aisa nahi karti.

User ki temporary information multiple requests ke beech maintain karne ke liye traditional Java web applications HttpSession ka use karti hain.


## WHAT IS HTTP SESSION ?
---------------------------

HTTP / HTTPS (HTTP + TLS/SSL) protocol by default stateless hota hai.

Matlab server ko by default ye yaad nahi rehta ki
current request kis user ne bheji hai aur kya us user ne
isse pehle koi request bheji thi.

Lekin real-world applications me hume user ki kuch information
multiple requests ke beech maintain karni padti hai.

HttpSession ek server-side object hai jo Servlet Container (e.g. Tomcat) provide karta hai, 
taaki user ki temporary session-related information ko multiple HTTP requests ke beech maintain kiya ja sake.
ya simple language me ek "container" hai jisme application user ke login ke
baad uski session-related information ko multiple HTTP
requests ke beech temporarily store karti hai.


User Login
    ↓
Tomcat creates HttpSession
    ↓
Application stores data inside HttpSession


Application pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. verify karti hai ki username/password ya credentials
valid hain ya nahi).


Authentication successful hone ke baad application requests the
Servlet Container (e.g. Tomcat) to create an HttpSession.
The Servlet Container creates the HttpSession object and the
application stores the required user-related information inside it.

Authentication typically involves verifying the user's credentials
against a Database, LDAP server, OAuth Provider, or another
Identity Provider, depending on the application's authentication mechanism.

Baad me aane wali requests (API requests) me isi session ki help se
user ko identify (Authentication Context) kiya jata hai,
aur uske Roles / Permissions ke basis par Authorization
perform ki ja sakti hai. 


HttpSession user-related temporary information
store kar sakta hai, jaise:

├── Logged-in User ID
├── Username
├── User Roles (ADMIN / USER)
├── Authentication Status
├── CSRF Token
├── Temporary OTP Verification State
├── Multi-step Form Data
├── User Preferences (Language, Theme)
└── Other temporary user-specific information


Isi purpose ke liye HttpSession use kiya jata hai.


## NOTE:
----------

Technically Shopping Cart jaise business data ko HttpSession
me store kiya ja sakta hai, lekin modern distributed applications
generally aisa avoid karti hain.

Business data (Cart, Wishlist, Orders, etc.) usually Database
me store hota hai, jabki HttpSession sirf temporary user context
(e.g. Logged-in User, Roles, Authentication Status) maintain
karne ke liye use hota hai.

Isliye HttpSession expire hone, server restart/crash hone ke baad bhi important business data available rehta hai.


## IMPORTANT:
---------------

HttpSession khud koi storage technology (jaise Redis,
Database, ya File System) nahi hai.

Ye sirf ek Session Object hai.

By default, Tomcat jaise Servlet Containers is Session Object
ko server ki local memory (RAM) me store karte hain.

Is approach ki kuch limitations hain:

1. Agar application multiple servers par deploy ho ?(i.e horizontally scalerd ho ),
   to default in-memory HttpSession Horizontal Scaling
   ke saath problems create kar sakta hai.

   Kyunki har server ki apni separate local memory hoti hai,
   isliye ek server par create hua HttpSession automatically
   doosre server ko available nahi hota.

2. Agar server restart ya crash ho jaye,
   to us server ki local memory clear ho jaati hai.

   Is wajah se saare in-memory HttpSessions destroy ho jaate hain,
   aur users ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.

Isi wajah se modern distributed applications
generally Stateless Authentication (JWT) ya
External Session Store (e.g. Redis) prefer karti hain.


## HOW DOES HTTP SESSION WORK ?
--------------------------------

Suppose user login karta hai.

Flow:

        User
          |
          | Login Request
          v
Spring Boot + Tomcat


Application (i.e Authentication framework like Spring Security) pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. verify karti hai ki username/password ya credentials
valid hain ya nahi).This can involve DB to verify the creds.

Authentication successful hone ke baad application
generally HttpSession create karti hai
(through the Servlet Container such as Tomcat).

Tomcat us session ko ek unique Session ID assign karta hai.

Ye mapping internally maintain ki jaati hai:

        Session ID
             ↓
     HttpSession Object


Tomcat internally ek Session Manager maintain karta hai,
jisme Session ID aur uske corresponding HttpSession Object
ki mapping stored hoti hai.


## EXAMPLE :
-------------

Session Store

|--------------------------------------------
|                                            |
| Session ID : ABC123XYZ                     |
|                                            |
|        |                                   |
|        v                                   |
|                                            |
|  HttpSession Object                        |
|                                            |
|    User = Anurag                           |
|    Role = USER                             |           
|    Last Access Time                        |
|    Creation Time                           |
|                                            |
 --------------------------------------------

Application HttpSession create karne ke liye
Servlet Container (e.g. Tomcat) ka use karti hai.

Servlet Container (Tomcat) actual HttpSession object
create karta hai and user ki session-related
information store karta hai.

By default, ye HttpSession server ki local
memory (RAM) me store hota hai.

Uske baad Tomcat browser ko response ke saath
ek cookie bhejta hai.

Cookie me poora session data nahi bheja jata. 
Sirf Session ID bheji jaati hai.

Example:

    JSESSIONID = ABC123XYZ

Browser is cookie ko store kar leta hai.

Subsequent requests me browser automatically check karta hai ki
stored cookie current request ke liye applicable hai ya nahi
(Domain, Path, Expiry, Secure, SameSite etc.).

Agar applicable ho, to browser automatically JSESSIONID cookie
request ke saath bhej deta hai.

Jab browser next request bhejta hai,

to browser automatically

    JSESSIONID = ABC123

Server ko bhej deta hai.

Tomcat apne Session Store me Session ID ko lookup
karta hai aur corresponding HttpSession Object
retrieve karta hai.

Uske baad application user ke Roles / Permissions
ke basis par Authorization perform kar sakti hai.

Isi wajah se user ko har request par dobara
login karne ki zarurat nahi padti.


## NOTE :
----------
Browser ke paas actual session data nahi hota.
Browser ke paas sirf Session ID hoti hai.


## EXAMPLE 1: REGULAR CART:
----------------------------

Suppose tumne kisi E-commerce application me login kiya,
jo HttpSession use karti hai.

Application pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. username/password ya other credentials verify karti hai).
Authentication ke liye required user information (e.g. User ID, Roles, Permissions)
generally Database ya kisi Identity Provider se retrieve ki jaati hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad application,
Servlet Container (e.g. Tomcat) se HttpSession create karwati hai.

Tomcat HttpSession create karta hai, usse server ki local memory (RAM) me store karta hai aur us HttpSession ko ek unique Session ID assign karta hai.
Uske baad application user ki required session-related information (e.g. User ID, Roles, Permissions) HttpSession me store kar deti hai.

Uske baad Tomcat response ke saath browser ko ek cookie bhejta hai jisme Session ID (e.g. JSESSIONID=ABC123XYZ) hoti hai.

Browser is cookie ko store kar leta hai.

Subsequent requests me agar cookie current request ke liye applicable ho,
to browser automatically JSESSIONID request ke saath bhej deta hai.

Ab jab user Orders, Profile ya Account page open karta hai,
to browser backend ko multiple API requests bhejta hai.

Application har request par user ke credentials ko dobara
Database ya kisi Identity Provider ke against verify nahi karti.

Iske bajay Tomcat Session ID ki help se corresponding
HttpSession retrieve karta hai.

Application us HttpSession se user ki information aur Roles
obtain karti hai aur unke basis par Authorization perform karti hai.

Agar user ke paas required permission hoti hai,
to request successfully process kar di jaati hai.


## EXAMPLE 2: ANONYMOUS CART
-----------------------------

Suppose tum Flipkart par bina login kiye products
cart me add karte ho.

Is time application ko user ki identity pata nahi hoti,
kyunki user abhi authenticate nahi hua hai.

Isliye application temporary shopping cart ko
HttpSession me store kar sakti hai.

Baad me jab user login karta hai,
to application us temporary cart ko user ke account se
associate karke Database me persist (store) kar deti hai.

Iske baad future requests me user apna cart
alag-alag devices ya browser sessions se bhi access
kar sakta hai.

Ye anonymous shopping cart implement karne ka
ek common approach hai.



## DOES HTTP SESSION EXPIRE? YA USER LIFETIME LOGIN REHTA HAI?
----------------------------------------------------------------

Haan.

HttpSession ka expiry time hota hai.

Generally session following situations me end ho sakti hai:

1. SESSION TIMEOUT (MOST COMMON)
---------------------------------

Agar user configured timeout period (e.g. 30 minutes) tak koi request nahi bhejta,

to Tomcat automatically HttpSession destroy kar deta hai.

Spring Boot Configuration:

    server.servlet.session.timeout=30m


2. USER LOGOUT
---------------

Jab user logout karta hai,

application

    session.invalidate();

call karke current HttpSession ko immediately
destroy kar deti hai.


3. SERVER RESTART
------------------

By default HttpSession ,server ki local memory (RAM)
me store hota hai.

Agar server restart ho jaye,

to RAM clear ho jaati hai,

aur saari HttpSessions bhi destroy ho jaata hain.


4. SERVER CRASH
----------------

Agar server crash ho jaye,

to RAM me stored saari HttpSessions
lost ho jaati hain.

Result:

User ko dobara login karna padta hai.



